WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:04.040
A lone whale,

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:05.260
adrift at sea,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.500
a million meters cubed of no response. He calls at the wrong frequency.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.880
Listen to the call of what is considered to be the loneliest whale in the world.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:26.500
[whale noises]

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.780
This call is at a frequency of 52 Hertz.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:37.919
This frequency is very unusual for a whale, with most whales calling at around 10-40 Hertz.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:45.539
This call comes from what appears to be the only whale in the ocean at this pitch.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.320
Now listen to the call of the blue whale to compare.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:01:02.640
[whale noises]

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.140
Both of these sounds have been speed up 10 times so that we can hear them easily and

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:12.949
humans are not the only ones with a hearing limit.

00:01:13.619 --> 00:01:15.619
Whales use sound to communicate,

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.250
to swim with others and to find a mate.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.479
It is thought that the sounds of the 52 Hertz whale

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:29.839
cannot be heard by any others and thus it is destined to forever swim alone.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.360
Singing out with no reply.

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:37.428
Ideas are that the whale could be malformed, a

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.169
hybrid between a blue whale and another species, or deaf, as

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:47.030
the sounds we make can be influenced by the sounds we hear.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:55.369
This is definitely a poignant story that makes us think about the projection of human emotions.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:57.949
We all feel isolated at times,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:06.710
familiar with the idea that despite our attempts, we are not heard or seen, that we are alone in a crowded sea.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:12.829
Does this whale feel loneliness? I don't know. How can we know?

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.750
Detected first in

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:19.160
1989 it has been swimming alone for over 25 years.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:27.079
If all it has ever known is solitude, would it still miss a crowd?

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.299
I would imagine a sense of peace comes with accepting that

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.440
life is just the cool water breaking over your fin,

00:02:37.049 --> 00:02:40.039
but humans would find that hard to accept.

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:48.199
Do you think you could still appreciate beauty in life if you had no one like you to share it with?

